<div class="Project-Modal" id="project-modal" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="Project-Modal-Backdrop"></div>

    <div class="Project-Book" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="project-book-title">
        <button class="Project-Modal-Close" type="button" aria-label="Close project details">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
                <path d="M6 6L18 18M18 6L6 18" />
            </svg>
        </button>

        <div class="Project-Book-Pages">
            <!-- INTERVAL -->
            <section class="Project-Details" data-project-details="interval" aria-hidden="true">
                <article class="Project-Page Project-Page--active">
                    <div class="Project-Page-Content Project-Cover">
                        <div>
                            <p class="Project-Page-Label"
                                style="color: rgb(255, 141, 35); text-decoration: overline underline; text-underline-position: under;">
                                ·Currently In Development·
                            </p>
                            <h2 id="project-book-title">Interval</h2>
                            <p class="Project-Cover-Subtitle">
                                Interactive ear-training platform
                            </p>

                            <ul class="Project-Stack">
                                <li>Next.js</li>
                                <li>React</li>
                                <li>TypeScript</li>
                                <li>Tone.js</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <img src="/images/art/graphics/interval-logo.svg" alt="Interval logo"
                            class="Project-Cover-Logo">
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="Project-Page">
                    <div class="Project-Page-Content">
                        <p class="Project-Page-Label">01 — Product Vision</p>
                        <div class="Page-Details">
                            <h3>Ear training made fun</h3>
                            <p>
                                As someone who enjoys creating and listening to music, I found many ear-training apps
                                repetitive, frustrating, or overpriced. Interval is designed to make music education
                                more engaging, accessible, and rewarding through a game-focused learning experience.
                            </p>
                            <div class="Project-List">
                                <div class="List-Title">
                                    <p>
                                        Built for the User:
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Personalized training</li>
                                    <li>Adaptive difficulty</li>
                                    <li>Cross-platform synchronization</li>
                                    <li>Meaningful progress tracking</li>
                                    <li>Interactive, game-like exercises</li>
                                    <li>Free access core games</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                        </div>


                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="Project-Page">
                    <div class="Project-Page-Content">
                        <p class="Project-Page-Label">02 — Training System </p>
                        <div class="Page-Details">
                            <h3>Practice Through Play</h3>
                            <p>
                                Interval is designed to make music education feel like progression rather than
                                repetition. Users can build consistent habits, advance through increasingly challenging
                                exercises, and strengthen different areas of their musical ear through focused,
                                game-like training.
                            </p>

                            <div class="Project-List">
                                <div class="List-Title">
                                    <p>
                                        Planned training experiences include:
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                                <div class="List-Subheading">
                                    <p>Core Game Modes</p>
                                </div>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Interval Recognition</li>
                                    <li>Pitch Training</li>
                                    <li>Chord Progression Recognition</li>
                                    <li>Hearing Harmonies</li>
                                    <li>Music Theory Basics Bootcamp</li>
                                </ul>
                                <div class="List-Subheading">
                                    <p>Progress Features</p>
                                </div>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Daily streaks and practice goals</li>
                                    <li>Skill levels and milestones</li>
                                    <li>Achievements and mastery badges</li>
                                    <li>Personalized practice recommendations</li>
                                    <li>Progress reports and weak-area tracking</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="Project-Page">
                    <div class="Project-Page-Content">
                        <p class="Project-Page-Label">03 — Engineering</p>
                        <div class="Page-Details">

                            <h3>Sound into Interaction</h3>

                            <p> Interval uses Tone.js to schedule notes and chords while React manages the current
                                question, selected answer, feedback state, difficulty level, and visual keyboard
                                updates. </p>

                            <p> The training system is built from reusable note and interval data, making it easier to
                                generate randomized exercises and expand the platform with additional difficulty levels
                                and game modes. </p>

                            <p> A key challenge was keeping the interface synchronized with asynchronous audio. I added
                                playback cancellation and state checks so users can move between questions without
                                delayed notes or feedback carrying into the next exercise. </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="Project-Page">
                    <div class="Project-Page-Content">
                        <p class="Project-Page-Label">04 — Reflection</p>
                        <div class="Page-Details">
                            <h3>Learn from Teaching</h3>

                            <p> Interval began as a single ear-training exercise, but it quickly became an opportunity
                                to think more deeply about how people learn. I had to consider pacing, feedback,
                                difficulty, and motivation alongside the technical implementation. </p>

                            <p> The project strengthened my ability to connect product decisions with engineering
                                choices. Reusable training logic, responsive audio, and clear visual feedback all became
                                part of creating an experience that felt consistent rather than just functional. </p>

                            <p> More than anything, Interval taught me to evaluate a project from the user’s perspective
                                and to treat accessibility, clarity, and engagement as core parts of the build. </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="Project-Page">
                    <div class="Project-Page-Content">
                        <p class="Project-Page-Label">05 — Next Steps</p>
                        <div class="Page-Details">
                            <h3>Now What?</h3>

                            <p>Interval is STILL in development.</p>

                            <p> The next phase of Interval is focused on expanding the platform beyond interval
                                recognition while strengthening the systems that support long-term learning. </p>

                            <div class="Project-List">
                                <div class="List-Title">
                                    <p>Planned development:</p>
                                </div>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Additional pitch, chord, harmony, and theory exercises</li>
                                    <li>Adaptive difficulty based on user performance</li>
                                    <li>Profiles with synchronized progress across devices</li>
                                    <li>Daily goals, streaks, achievements, and skill levels</li>
                                    <li>Performance reports and weak-area recommendations</li>
                                    <li>Improved accessibility and mobile responsiveness</li>
                                </ul>
                            </div>

                            <p> These additions will turn the current training experience into a broader system that can
                                guide users from basic recognition skills toward more advanced musical understanding.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </article>
            </section>
            <!-- CAPSTONE -->
